## Feature #7 Increment 2 (spec §4.7) — `TestClock.set(d)`: set virtual-now to an
## ABSOLUTE instant (`d` ns since the virtual epoch), firing every timer at/
## before it. Time never moves backwards — a `set` below the current virtual-now
## is clamped (no rewind).
##
## A sleep at 30s is registered (hand-poll parks it). `set(20s)` is BEFORE its
## deadline → does NOT fire it (still pending). `set(40s)` is PAST it → fires it
## (ready). A final `set(10s)` (backwards) is clamped — no rewind, no spurious
## state. Proves absolute set + the no-rewind clamp.

use std.time.Duration

def main
  puts "set start"
  var guard = TestClock.install
  var cx = Context.executor

  var s = Async.sleep(Duration.from_secs(30))
  match (&var s).poll(&var cx)
    Poll.Ready(_) -> puts "p1=ready (BUG)"
    Poll.Pending  -> puts "p1=pending"
  end

  # Absolute set to 20s — before the 30s deadline → not yet fired.
  TestClock.set(Duration.from_secs(20))
  match (&var s).poll(&var cx)
    Poll.Ready(_) -> puts "p2=ready (BUG: 20s < 30s deadline)"
    Poll.Pending  -> puts "p2=pending"
  end

  # Absolute set to 40s — past the deadline → fires.
  TestClock.set(Duration.from_secs(40))
  match (&var s).poll(&var cx)
    Poll.Ready(_) -> puts "p3=ready"
    Poll.Pending  -> puts "p3=pending (BUG: 40s >= 30s deadline)"
  end

  # Backwards set to 10s — clamped (no rewind). Nothing changes.
  TestClock.set(Duration.from_secs(10))
  puts "set done"
end
